WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.519
Hi, I'm Mullion!

00:00:02.519 --> 00:00:05.755
Here is how coding agents lay out a request so the provider

00:00:05.755 --> 00:00:07.104
can skip most of it.

00:00:10.642 --> 00:00:14.071
A provider only reuses work for a prefix it has seen before.

00:00:14.071 --> 00:00:17.501
So the harness keeps the start of the request still, and appends

00:00:17.501 --> 00:00:18.929
every change at the end.

00:00:20.508 --> 00:00:22.980
Every request opens with the same blocks.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:26.512
The tool definitions, the system prompt, and the project memory.

00:00:26.932 --> 00:00:30.069
Then the conversation, which only ever grows, and the newest turn

00:00:30.069 --> 00:00:31.210
at the very end.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:34.850
A cache marker on the last block tells the provider to save

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:36.192
everything up to that point.

00:00:36.612 --> 00:00:40.421
On the next request, the whole old prefix comes back as a cheap read.

00:00:40.421 --> 00:00:42.325
Only the new turn is fresh work.

00:00:43.942 --> 00:00:47.224
Write the date into the system prompt, or add a tool, and

00:00:47.224 --> 00:00:49.959
every token after that point has to be processed again.

00:00:50.379 --> 00:00:52.206
The newer harnesses append instead.

00:00:52.206 --> 00:00:56.225
Codex, pi and opencode put updates after the history, so the

00:00:56.225 --> 00:00:57.686
old prefix still matches.

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:01.494
Anthropic bills a request in bands.

00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:04.347
Tokens up to the highest hit are cache reads.

00:01:04.347 --> 00:01:05.933
After that come cache writes.

00:01:06.353 --> 00:01:09.606
When nothing before the marker changed, the whole prefix is a read.

00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:14.582
My cost model for one session on Sonnet four point six.

00:01:14.582 --> 00:01:17.718
A stable layout costs about fifteen percent of no caching.

00:01:17.718 --> 00:01:21.481
A timestamp at the top costs more than no caching at all.

00:01:22.975 --> 00:01:26.194
So keep the start of the request still for the whole session,

00:01:26.194 --> 00:01:28.340
and put anything that changes after the history.

00:01:31.608 --> 00:01:33.586
Prefix caches match from the start.

00:01:33.586 --> 00:01:36.222
Put stable blocks first, and append the changes.

00:01:36.642 --> 00:01:38.795
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:38.795 --> 00:01:39.410
I'm Mullion.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:39.718
Bye!
